  "cause": "On 22 September 2026, **the foreign ministers of the G7 — the UK, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States — issued a joint statement demanding that Iran \"end its arming of and support\" for the Houthis in Yemen, condemning Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia \"in the strongest terms,\" and declaring that the escalation against Saudi-backed pro-government forces \"represented a threat to regional and global energy security\"** (BBC). The statement, issued after a meeting on the UNGA sidelines, called on the Houthis to \"immediately cease all military actions, all threats and attacks against civilian shipping, and to return to the political process in good faith,\" and warned that their actions \"constitute a dangerous pattern of escalation that risks further exacerbating the conflict, undermining international trade and creating global economic instability.\" Iran has previously denied supplying weapons to the Houthis, saying it supports them only politically (BBC). The statement followed **a British decision to answer a Saudi request for \"military support,\"** agreed by Prime Minister Andy Burnham on the advice of Defence Secretary Wes Streeting and Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband; a diplomatic source told AFP that France has pledged support too, saying it is ready to work on \"securing\" Saudi energy infrastructure via a \"strictly defensive approach\" (BBC). **Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman has also requested military support from President Trump, who has wavered for weeks over whether to act, and who told Fox News he was in \"deciding mode\" on the next steps in the war against Iran** (NYT, BBC). The battlefield accounting has worsened on both sides. Houthi-run Saba news agency reported that **a Saudi pre-dawn strike on a house in the Red Sea port city of Mokha on Tuesday killed six people, including two children and a woman**, and that nine detainees were killed in an attack on a prison in al-Hazm, in Jawf province, on Monday night; the Houthis' health ministry said two members of one family were killed in a strike on Jawf and one person was killed in Taiz (BBC). Government-run media reported that civilians were injured in a Houthi missile attack on a market in the Mudaribah wa al-Ara district of Lahj province, about 20km east of Houthi-controlled territory, with verified CCTV showing a munition hitting an area crowded with people and vehicles. The Saudi-led coalition has not commented (BBC). The Houthis took control of Bab al-Mandab — the chokepoint where the southern Red Sea meets the Gulf of Aden — earlier this month, and Saudi Arabia has retaliated with hundreds of air strikes while the Houthis have fired hundreds of drones and missiles at Saudi cities and infrastructure including Riyadh (BBC). Meanwhile the United Nations warned of rising displacement as thousands more flee toward Djibouti (Al Jazeera, Reuters). And in the economic register the first repairs have begun: **Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline, which carries oil to the Red Sea, has restarted after drone attacks, and Riyadh has told Asian refiners they can soon pick up crude on the Red Sea; oil prices fell for a fourth day as supply concerns eased**, moving little after Trump's UN remarks (qz, Bloomberg, WSJ, CNBC). **The cause is a war over a chokepoint in which the world's oil trade is being rerouted around the fighting faster than anyone is negotiating about the fighting.**",
