  "hinge": "The reading that matters here is the one Sinkevičius and Estonia's foreign minister both offered and no one can yet confirm — that Putin is using the appearance of negotiation to buy time. If that reading is right, then the corrective is not louder deterrence but *verifiable* restraint: promises carried out, on a schedule, where both sides can see them. A pledge to Ukraine and to the Baltics is either kept or it teaches every adversary what a pledge is worth. Change that one variable — make the word of the great power auditable and matched by actual capability on the ground, rather than announced to be delivered in 2027 — and the entire incentive structure that rewards stalling collapses. §49 is unforgiving about what follows when it does not: the promise that is not kept is not merely a broken commitment, it is a subsidy paid to whoever is deciding whether to test it."
