  "verse": "…you make your home planet into a shadow world (hell) and place of existence for a limited time, because you wreak destruction on the appearance (nature) and destroy your world through battles (wars) and hatred as well as through jealousy, through pathological craving for revenge and pathological craving for retaliation and through everything evil.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 7, Verse 25",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the typhoon. It is the conjunction of two human seams that have to hold for the loss to be governed: the margins governments keep between a correct warning and an unheeded one, and the emissions that set the ocean's starting temperature. The first is a decision about land use and mandatory evacuation, and it is available today — Japan had the forecast, the shelters and the 1.9 million alerts, and came away with deaths concentrated in landslides whose zones are mapped in advance. The second is a decision stretched over decades and is now negotiated in the same building Dujuan's damage is being tallied in. §25 does not describe unavoidable nature; it describes what people do to the appearance of the world, and among the acts it lists, \"destroying your world\" is a single item on a list of decisions. The storm cannot be changed. Both of the causes that made it a verdict can be."
